### 2. Meldefonds vs. Non-Reporting Funds

Austria classifies investment funds into two main tax categories:

| Parameter | Reporting Fund (*Meldefonds*) | Non-Reporting Fund (*Nicht-Meldefonds*) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Tax Reporting** | The fund appoints a fiscal representative in Austria who submits detailed annual tax data to **Oesterreichische Kontrollbank (OeKB)**. | No annual tax data is provided to OeKB. |
| **Taxation Base** | Tax is calculated on **actual** distributions and actual deemed distribution income (*ausschüttungsgleiche Erträge*). | Tax is calculated on a **penalty lump-sum base** (*Pauschalbesteuerung*). |
| **Lump-Sum Base Rate** | N/A | **90% of the annual increase in net asset value (NAV)**, with a guaranteed statutory minimum of **10% of the fund’s year-end NAV**. |
| **Tax Rate** | 27.5% KESt on actual income. | 27.5% KESt applied to the lump-sum estimate. |
| **Investor Impact** | Precise taxation; historical acquisition costs are adjusted upward to prevent double taxation on sale. | Severe over-taxation risk, administrative burden, potential double taxation, and delayed tax corrections. |

#### How to Verify Reporting Status

Before purchasing any ETF or fund, check its status in the official public database:

* **Official Registry:** [OeKB Profit-Web Fund Database](https://www.google.com/search?q=https://my.oekb.at/profit-web/).
* **Verification Process:** Search by the fund’s **ISIN**. If the fund appears with an active tax status (showing historical tax notifications / *Meldungen*), it is a *Meldefonds*. If it is missing or explicitly marked as non-reporting, avoid buying it.

